body:
This change tightens validation in the virtio-net RX path to reduce the amount of state we retain while processing guest-provided descriptor chains.

The current flow batches queue elements and defers completion bookkeeping until the full packet has been assembled. While functional, that approach keeps more uncommitted state alive across the receive loop and broadens the surface for malformed or adversarial descriptor layouts to influence error handling.

This update hardens the boundary by:
- completing descriptors as soon as they have been fully validated and consumed;
- avoiding temporary per-packet arrays used to track queued elements and lengths;
- simplifying error paths so malformed input is rejected immediately at the point of detection;
- preserving existing checks for missing buffers / insufficient descriptors while reducing intermediate state.

From a security perspective, this is a defensive cleanup: less deferred state, fewer objects carried across loop iterations, and earlier validation/completion all help make the RX path more robust against unexpected queue contents from the guest side.

No functional behavior is intended to change for valid traffic; this is a hardening-oriented refactor of input handling in the receive path.

